NEXUS TERMINAL — Universal Polyglot Runtime - The Smallest Sovereign Language Host
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Certificate of Capability
=========================

Project    : Nexus Terminal
Codename   : Genesis
Version    : 1.0.0
Build      : 2026.10.16
Author     : Aissa Mohammedi (DGK)
Protocol   : Nametron 3 Lara / ZTMESH v1.5.15
License    : NEXUS-OPEN-2.0

This document certifies that Nexus Terminal is a sovereign, self-contained
runtime environment capable of identifying, launching, and orchestrating
programs written in every major programming language in existence.

Features:
--------

- UNIVERSAL! Nexus Terminal detects and executes code from 40+ programming
  languages without external configuration. Drop a file, type a command,
  the language runs. No manifest required, no config file, no ceremony.

- SOVEREIGN! All language runtimes are managed locally. No cloud dependency,
  no telemetry, no telemetry relay. Every interpreter, compiler, or VM
  invoked by Nexus Terminal runs on the host machine, under your control.

- AUTONOMOUS! The terminal identifies the language of a file from its
  shebang, extension, or content signature — then routes execution through
  the correct runtime automatically. You never specify the language twice.

- COMPLETE! Includes native support for interpreted languages (Python,
  Ruby, Perl, PHP, JavaScript, Lua, Tcl, R, Julia), compiled languages
  (C, C++, Rust, Go, Zig, Nim, D, Swift, Objective-C), JVM languages
  (Java, Kotlin, Scala, Groovy, Clojure), .NET languages (C#, F#, VB.NET),
  functional languages (Haskell, OCaml, Erlang, Elixir, F#, Scheme, Lisp),
  systems languages (Assembly x86/ARM/RISC-V), and scripting languages
  (Bash, Zsh, Fish, PowerShell, AWK, sed).

- SCRIPTABLE! Any source file can be made executable by adding a shebang
  header. Nexus Terminal respects the POSIX shebang contract and additionally
  supports inline invocation of any registered runtime via its `run`
  directive. Your C code becomes a script. Your Rust code becomes a script.
  Your Java code becomes a script.

- FAST! Language detection is O(1). Runtime resolution is cached per-file.
  Repeated invocations of the same language reuse the resolved interpreter
  path without re-scanning the filesystem.

- SAFE! Every invocation runs in a sandboxed context by default — restricted
  filesystem access, no network unless explicitly enabled, resource limits
  enforced. The `--unsafe` flag opts out, and is never the default.

- EXTENSIBLE! The language registry is a plain-text manifest at
  ~/.config/nexus/languages.toml. Adding a language is a five-line entry.
  No recompilation, no restart, no magic.

Documentation:
-------------

1) Installation

   git clone https://github.com/Aissamohammedi88/nexus-terminal
   cd nexus-terminal
   ./configure
   make
   make install

   By default, Nexus Terminal is installed in /usr/local/bin/nexus.
   ./configure --help shows configuration options including:
     --prefix         Install prefix (default: /usr/local)
     --with-runtimes  Comma-separated list of language runtimes to enable
     --without-cloud  Disable all cloud provider integrations
     --with-shield    Enable UNION SHIELD integration
     --with-tor       Enable Tor routing for outbound connections

   System requirements:
     - Linux 5.10+ / macOS 12+ / BSD variants / WSL2
     - 512 MB RAM minimum, 4 GB recommended
     - Python 3.10+ (bootstrap only)
     - Optional: Node.js 18+, Go 1.20+, Rust 1.70+ for their respective
       language runtimes


2) Introduction

   Nexus Terminal is not a shell. It is a language-agnostic execution host.
   Where bash answers to `bash`, where Python answers to `python3`, where
   gcc answers to `gcc`, Nexus Terminal answers to all of them — through a
   single, unified command: `nexus run`.

   The terminal maintains an internal registry of every programming language
   installed on the host system. On first invocation, it performs a full
   scan of the PATH and known runtime locations, indexing:
     - Interpreter and compiler binaries
     - Default file extensions
     - Recognized shebang signatures
     - Version strings
     - Common aliases

   This index is cached locally and refreshed on demand via `nexus refresh`.

   The user invokes a file:
       nexus run script.py
       nexus run main.rs
       nexus run app.go
       nexus run worker.ex

   Nexus Terminal resolves the runtime, executes the file with the correct
   arguments and environment, captures stdout/stderr, and returns the result.
   The user never types `python3` again. The user never types `cargo run`
   again. The user types `nexus run`.

   For files marked executable with a shebang, the invocation is even
   shorter:
       ./script.py
       ./main.rs
       ./app.go
       ./worker.ex

   Any of these can begin with the universal Nexus shebang:
       #!/usr/local/bin/nexus run

   And Nexus Terminal will resolve the correct runtime automatically.


