6) Architecture

   Nexus Terminal is composed of seven logical layers:

     Layer 1 — Detection
       Filesystem walker, shebang parser, extension matcher,
       content signature analyzer.

     Layer 2 — Registry
       Local index of installed runtimes. Cached at
       ~/.cache/nexus/languages.db (SQLite).

     Layer 3 — Resolver
       Maps detected language to runtime binary path.
       Handles version selection, alias resolution,
       environment setup.

     Layer 4 — Executor
       Spawns the correct process. Captures I/O.
       Manages timeouts, signals, resource limits.

     Layer 5 — Sandbox
       Applies seccomp, namespaces, rlimits.
       Blocks network unless --unsafe.
       Restricts filesystem to project root.

     Layer 6 — Telemetry
       Local-only metrics. Never transmitted.
       Accessible via `nexus stats`.

     Layer 7 — Integration
       Hooks into UNION SHIELD, Tor, Gina AI,
       GitHub Actions, CI/CD pipelines.


7) Security

   Nexus Terminal enforces a strict security model by default:

   - All executions run in a sandboxed namespace.
   - Network access is blocked unless explicitly enabled.
   - Filesystem access is restricted to the project directory.
   - Environment variables from the host are not inherited.
   - Secrets are never logged, never cached, never transmitted.
   - The `--unsafe` flag is required to disable any of the above.
   - UNION SHIELD can be integrated to add kernel-level monitoring.

   See docs/security.md for the full threat model and mitigation matrix.


8) Performance

   Language detection:      < 1 ms (cached) / < 50 ms (cold)
   Runtime resolution:      < 1 ms (cached) / < 200 ms (cold)
   Process spawn overhead:  < 10 ms
   Total invocation:        < 100 ms for cached languages

   Comparison with individual tool invocation:
     python3 script.py        = 45 ms + script execution
     nexus run script.py      = 55 ms + script execution
     overhead                 = ~10 ms (acceptable for the convenience)

   For scripts longer than 500 ms, overhead is negligible.
   For short scripts, use `nexus run --fast` to skip sandbox setup.


